description:
crate-jmap-calendars-server has TWO `seed_object` methods with logically identical contracts but divergent signatures:

- memory.rs:356-404 — `MemoryBackend::seed_object(&self, account_id: &str, type_name: &'static str, id: &str, value: serde_json::Value)`
- lib.rs:341-382 — `test_support::MockBackend::seed_object(&self, account_id: &str, type_name: &str, id: &str, value: serde_json::Value)`

Both run the same runtime assertion against the same hard-coded `KNOWN_TYPES = ["Calendar", "CalendarEvent", "CalendarEventNotification", "ParticipantIdentity"]` list to catch typos. The `&'static str` bound on MemoryBackend adds no safety the assert does not already provide; it just forbids callers from passing computed type names.

Concretely:

```rust
// Works on MockBackend, fails to compile on MemoryBackend:
let prefix = "Calendar";
let kind = format!("{prefix}Event");
backend.seed_object("acc1", &kind, "id1", json!({"id": "id1"}));
//                          ^^^^ &String does not coerce to &'static str
```

A test author writing a parameterised fixture across all four type names must either hard-code each call site or restrict themselves to the `MockBackend` form. The constraint surfaces as a paper cut at the moment a test author tries to factor out their setup code.

The fix is a one-line change on MemoryBackend: `type_name: &'static str` → `type_name: &str`. The function body already handles `&str` correctly (the KNOWN_TYPES slice is `&[&'static str]` so `KNOWN_TYPES.contains(&type_name)` works for any `&str`). The runtime assertion is unchanged. The signature change is non-breaking for any caller currently passing literals.

`#[non_exhaustive]` is not relevant here — this is a free-function signature concern, not a struct field. Once the crate ships its first 0.x.0, relaxing `&'static str` to `&str` remains non-breaking (every existing caller with a literal compiles unchanged) so this isn't a SemVer-lock concern. Labeled `opinion` because the engineering basis is "consistency with the sibling-within-same-crate method" rather than "a caller cannot do their job"; reasonable people may consider the `'static` bound a deliberate "literal-only" cue and prefer leaving it.

Per workspace AGENTS.md "Canonical Templates" rule, the canonical jmap-mail-server is the template. If `jmap-mail-server::MemoryBackend::seed_object` exists and uses `&str` (likely — would need verification), this crate's `&'static str` is the divergence. If it does not exist, this is a calendars-only choice that the workspace pattern hasn't yet decided.
